  "summary": "Fins, atoms and jello — the future when splitting the atom was daydreaming.",
  "description": "Atompunk names the retrofuture of the atomic era: tailfins and chrome, starburst clocks, googie diners, bubble helmets, jello-and-casserole domestic futurism, and the general visual language from 1945-1965 when nuclear promise (not yet dread) powered optimism. The \"-punk\" suffix — applied by analogy to cyberpunk — formalized it as a design genre alongside dieselpunk and steampunk; games (Fallout), comics (Minutemen), TV (The Incredibles, Hello Tomorrow!) and collectors (orphan googie signage) sustain its aesthetics. Its signature motifs: atom orbit diagrams, blast-lamp silhouettes, and starbursts wherever ornament is wanted.",
  "culturalContext": "Atompunk names the retrofuture of the atomic era — tailfins, starburst clocks, googie diners and nuclear optimism from 1945-1965 — formalized as a '-punk' design genre alongside cyberpunk. Games (Fallout), TV (Hello Tomorrow!) and collectors sustain it as mid-century America's most confident visual future.",
